<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kot massacre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1846">1846</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kot massacre took place on the night of 14–15 September 1846 in the Kot, the palace armoury courtyard in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>. After the murder of a favourite of Queen Rajya <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmi">Lakshmi</a>, Jang Bahadur Kunwar and his brothers killed dozens of leading nobles and officials, including the prime minister. The massacre brought Jang Bahadur to power and began the rule of the hereditary Rana prime ministers, which lasted until 1951.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kot-massacre.jpg" alt="Kot massacre" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Saugatpoudel · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kotparva.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The massacre came at the peak of instability in Nepalese politics. In September 1845 a coalition ministry was formed under Fateh Jung Chautaria, but the real power behind the throne was General Gagan Singh Bhandari, who controlled seven regiments of the army against the prime minister's three. Abhiman Singh Rana Magar and Jung Bahadur Kunwar each commanded three regiments. Because King Rajendra Bikram Shah was held to be mentally unfit to rule, Queen Rajya Laxmi Devi handled affairs of state on his behalf, and it was occasionally alleged that she had an illicit relationship with Gagan Singh.</p><p>When Gagan Singh was found dead at his palace, the queen ordered Jung Bahadur, whose regiments were ready, to assemble the whole administrative establishment at once in the courtyard of the palace armoury at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanuman-dhoka">Hanuman Dhoka</a>. Jung Bahadur told his men to let people in but not out without the queen's orders or his own.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The courtiers hurried to the Kot, most of them carrying only a sword, and Jung Bahadur's troops took many of the arms they had brought. The queen blamed the Pande family for the murder and demanded that Abhiman Singh Rana Magar execute Kaji Bir Keshar Pande. Abhiman Singh hesitated, and the king said the guilty should be punished only after a proper investigation. Rajendra then left for the British residency and, refused an audience there, went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narayanhiti-palace">Narayanhiti Palace</a>, where he met the prime minister, who afterwards came to the Kot with little protection.</p><p>Abhiman Singh Rana Magar tried to leave to bring his own regiments, was stopped, and was killed as he tried to force his way out. Panic followed and the killing began. Many members of the Thapa, Pande and Basnyat families died, among them Fateh Jung, Khadga Bikram Shah and Dalbhanjan Pande. Some escaped over walls and roofs or through the drains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The queen named Jung Bahadur prime minister immediately afterwards, but soon plotted to remove him. The plot, known as the Basnyat Conspiracy, was betrayed and its leaders executed in the Bhandarkhal massacre. An assembly packed with his supporters found the queen guilty of complicity and sent her into exile at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> with the king. In 1847 Jung Bahadur declared Rajendra deposed and raised his son to the throne as Surendra Bikram Shah. Rajendra was captured in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terai">Terai</a> that year and spent the rest of his life under house arrest at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaktapur">Bhadgaon</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kot massacre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bhimsen Thapa dominated the government as mukhtiyar from 1806 to 1837, and the struggles between noble factions culminated in the <b>Kot massacre</b> of September 1846, when Jang Bahadur Kunwar and his brothers killed dozens of leading courtiers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
